d, while the strong Democratic environment and a lone, dated tie poll keep this short of Safe Republican.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling showing Pericola within single digits, or a Democratic wave running hotter than current national conditions, would put the seat in play.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 90 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Daniel Webster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 11th District is an open Republican-held seat in the Orlando exurbs, where Daniel Webster's retirement leaves the GOP field clear behind Joe Strada, who survived a recount to win a contested primary. The district leans Republican by about seven points, and Strada has raised roughly $5.5 million to Democrat James Pericola's $226,000, with outside groups adding to his edge. A Libertarian candidate is also on the ballot.\n\nRaters across the spectrum place the seat in the Safe-to-Likely Republican range, and prediction markets give Pericola about a one-in-eight chance. The strongest counterweight is a favorable national environment for Democrats this year, and a July poll sponsored by a Democratic firm showed the race tied, though it is dated and lacks published sample details. On balance the evidence points to a Republican hold, with the open-seat nature and the environment keeping it short of safe.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1071, "margin": -7.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1312, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1199, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0703, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0989, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1239, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0883, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1092, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0593, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.113